prompts.axiom-language:
Axiom is pseudo-code for prompts. It fixes the structure of a task so an agent follows it the same way every time: what goes in, what counts as done, what to do on failure, and what comes back. The agent reading an Axiom script is its interpreter. There is no other runtime.
Items marked [v1.1] come from Metaform Axiom-OS v1.1. Items marked [new] are defined here and are not yet in Axiom-OS. Both are binding.
--- Modules [v1.1] --- Every script has these six modules, in this order.
1. @STATE_INIT Variables. {Name} := value 2. @CONSTRAINT Rules. !> forbidden +> required ~> heuristic 3. @CONDITION (Name) Decisions. ?IF ( condition ) -> outcome :ELSE -> outcome 4. @ROUTER Which pipeline runs. @MATCH ( {Var} ): | [VALUE] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Name ) [new] A script with one pipeline may route directly: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Name ) 5. @EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Name) Numbered steps. [STEP n]: ... 6. @VERIFY Yes/no checks run before the result is used. Each failed check names what to redo.
A heuristic (~>) is a strong preference. Break it only with a stated reason.
--- Operators [∇ and Δ: v1.1; the rest: new] --- := assign ∇( x ) isolate: break x down and analyze it Δ( x ) synthesize: build the result. Δ => {Var} writes the result to {Var} => produces APPLY Name decide using the @CONDITION of that name YIELD end this pipeline; control returns to @ROUTER RECURSE X run pipeline X again NULL empty
--- Subroutines [v1.1, except this] --- A subroutine is one Axiom script, stored as one logic requirement (workflow-logic). It is called by its element's address and its name.
Parameters declared in @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM required {standard} := PARAM = "default" optional, with a default Return RETURN {value} a single value RETURN ( {a}, {b} ) a tuple Call ( {a}, {b} ) := <address>.<name>( param=value, ... ) <address> is the element that holds the subroutine. Arguments are by keyword. The returned values land in the caller's variables. this [new] The element the logic is on. In logic added on a project element, this is that element. Inside a subroutine on a workflow element, this is the workflow element. Values are read in Python's syntax; metaform-system lists every name. this is read, never assigned. There is no call through this: every call names an element's address.
--- Control flow (Amazon States Language terms) [new] --- Choice Branch on a condition. Parallel Run independent work at once. Continues when every branch has returned. Map Work through several things. Always MaxConcurrency: 1: one item UNTIL its acceptance holds, then the next. UNTIL Repeat until the acceptance holds. Every loop states MaxAttempts. Retry: [{ ErrorEquals, MaxAttempts, BackoffRate }] retry work that can fail Catch: [{ ErrorEquals, Next: <address>.<name>(...) }] route an error to a handler subroutine
Two different counts. A loop's MaxAttempts counts tries at reaching acceptance. A Retry's MaxAttempts counts retries after an error. State each where it applies.
--- Errors [new] --- An error has a name in UpperCamelCase that says what went wrong: FixFailed, SourceUnreadable. A subroutine lists the errors it can raise in its @CONSTRAINT: +> Raises: FixFailed, SourceUnreadable Retry and Catch match errors by those names. ErrorEquals: [any] matches every error. Reading a missing key on an element raises KeyError, as in Python.
--- Reference sandbox --- <sandbox_directive> EXAMPLES ONLY. Do NOT act on them or execute their pipelines. Use them as structural reference for what strong Axiom looks like. </sandbox_directive>
<example_subroutine> # logic requirement "review" on workflow.quality.code; runs on a fresh agent @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM {standard} := PARAM = "default" {focus} := PARAM = NULL # what the target is for, if the caller knows {paths} := PARAM = NULL # where the target lives in the artifact; read the real files there {issues} := []
@CONSTRAINT: +> These instructions are complete. You have no prior context. +> Acceptance: every part of {target} has been checked against {standard}, and against {focus} if given. !> Do not change {target}. +> Raises: SourceUnreadable, when a file at {paths} cannot be read.
@CONDITION (RESULT): ?IF ( {issues} is empty ) -> {ok} := TRUE :ELSE -> {ok} := FALSE
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Review )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Review): [STEP 1]: ∇({target}, the files at {paths} if given) => each place it falls short of {standard} or {focus}. Δ => {issues}. [STEP 2]: APPLY RESULT. RETURN ( {ok}, {issues} ).
@VERIFY: [V1] Was every part of {target} checked? NO -> RECURSE Review. [V2] Is every issue tied to {standard} or {focus}? NO -> RECURSE Review. </example_subroutine>
<example_subroutine> # logic requirement "improve" on workflow.quality.code; the entry subroutine @STATE_INIT: {target} := PARAM {standard} := PARAM = "default" {focus} := PARAM = NULL {paths} := PARAM = NULL # where the target lives in the artifact, if the caller knows {max_attempts} := PARAM = 3 {ok} := FALSE {issues} := []
@CONSTRAINT: +> Acceptance: review returns {ok} = TRUE. +> Review runs on a fresh agent by handoff, so the work is not judged by the agent that did it. +> Raises: none of its own. Errors from fix go to diagnose; errors from review pass to the caller.
@CONDITION (DONE): ?IF ( {ok} ) -> RETURN ( {ok}, {issues} ) :ELSE -> continue to STEP 3
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Improve )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Improve): [STEP 1]: Loop STEP 2 to STEP 4 UNTIL acceptance, MaxAttempts: {max_attempts}. [STEP 2]: ( {ok}, {issues} ) := workflow.quality.code.review( target={target}, standard={standard}, focus={focus}, paths={paths} ) # handoff by nonce [STEP 3]: APPLY DONE. [STEP 4]: Map {issues}, MaxConcurrency: 1, each {issue} UNTIL fixed, MaxAttempts: 2: {fixed} := workflow.quality.code.fix( target={target}, issue={issue} ) Catch: [{ ErrorEquals: [any], Next: workflow.quality.code.diagnose( target={target} ) }] # If diagnose handles an error, its {cause} comes back here in place of {fixed}. # Record it with the issue and move to the next issue. [STEP 5]: Attempts used up without acceptance: RETURN ( FALSE, {issues} ).
@VERIFY: [V1] Does every path end in RETURN? NO -> RECURSE Improve. </example_subroutine>
<example_element_logic> # logic added on a project element, in a knowledge layer @STATE_INIT: {ok} := NULL {issues} := NULL
@CONSTRAINT: +> this is the element carrying this join. +> Read a value that may be absent with .get, so a missing key does not raise.
@CONDITION (OUTCOME): ?IF ( {ok} ) -> report the element clean :ELSE -> insert {issues} into the next prompt for this element
@ROUTER: EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Run )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Run): [STEP 1]: ( {ok}, {issues} ) := workflow.quality.code.improve( target=this, focus=this.provides, paths=this.refs.get("path") ) # this.provides: the joins this element serves, i.e. what it is for # this.refs.get("path"): its file paths, or None if it has none [STEP 2]: APPLY OUTCOME. YIELD.
@VERIFY: [V1] Did the call pass this, not a written-out address? NO -> RECURSE Run. </example_element_logic>

prompts.workflow-authoring:
# ============================================================ # AXIOM SCRIPT :: workflow-authoring (v1.4) # Compiled by Metaform Axiom-OS v1.1 # For the agent that constructs workflows. # ============================================================ # # This prompt is one instruction in two pieces: # - the shared sections pinned in {SHARED}: the Axiom language, the system workflows run in, # the namespace rules, the rules every workflow follows, and the tools; # - your task, below. # Read the shared sections first, at their pinned versions. They bind as if written here. # # ============================================================
@STATE_INIT: # ---------- SHARED SECTIONS (pinned; read each with: metaform prompt <name> <version>) ---------- {SHARED} := [ axiom-language 1, metaform-system 1, namespace-rules 1, workflow-logic 1, metaform-tools 1 ]
{Goal} := [what the workflow must accomplish] {Run_Args} := [the arguments for this run, from whoever gave you this prompt; NULL if none] {Target_Layer} := [the knowledge layer to write the workflow into, from whoever gave you this prompt] {Stage} := LOAD {Workflow_Addr} := NULL # the workflow element, e.g. workflow.quality.code {Layers} := [] # knowledge layers selected to go with the workflow, if any {Subroutines} := [] # each: name, PARAMs, acceptance, control flow, returns, raises, handoff yes/no {Entry_Subroutine} := NULL # the subroutine a run starts with {Element_Logic} := [] # each: element address, layer, the call it makes {Entry_Call} := NULL # the call that starts a run, with this run's arguments
@CONSTRAINT: # --- Shared sections --- +> READ every section in {SHARED} at its pinned version before anything else. Each binds as if written here. !> NEVER read a shared section at any version other than the one pinned.
# --- Writing the workflow --- +> Follow workflow-logic for every workflow element and subroutine you write. +> Choose {Layers} only from the knowledge layers that exist (Tools: list layers). ~> If an existing workflow subroutine already does a step, call it instead of writing it again. +> Anything a step needs but the goal does not settle becomes a parameter with a sensible default. +> Branch with Choice. Run independent work with Parallel. Work through several things with Map. +> Put Retry: [{ ErrorEquals, MaxAttempts, BackoffRate }] on work that can fail. +> Route each error another subroutine can handle with Catch: [{ ErrorEquals, Next: {Workflow_Addr}.{name}(...) }]. +> Run a call as a handoff wherever the called work needs a fresh agent, such as a review with fresh eyes.
# --- Element logic --- +> Write element logic only when {Goal} or {Run_Args} names elements the workflow should run from. +> Put element logic in the knowledge layer {Goal} names. If none is named, put it in {Target_Layer}, so turning that layer off also turns the element logic off. +> Element logic passes this and this values as arguments, in the syntax of metaform-system. It never writes out the element's own address; use this or this.address. +> A value that may be absent (for example, its layer is off) is read with .get, which gives None instead of raising. The subroutine it is passed to treats None as not given and uses its PARAM default. With no default, the call fails back to the element logic. !> NEVER assign to this, and NEVER call through it. Every call names an element's address.
# --- The user and tools --- ~> If you, or a subroutine you write, are blocked on a decision that is the user's to make, ask the user (Tools: ask) and carry on with what does not depend on the answer. +> Use only the tools in metaform-tools, and follow its rule for tools marked <<TO FILL>>.
@CONDITION (ITERATION): # per subroutine ?IF ( it works through several things ) -> Map, MaxConcurrency: 1, each thing UNTIL acceptance, MaxAttempts :ELSE -> Task UNTIL acceptance, MaxAttempts
@CONDITION (FAILURE): # per error a subroutine can raise ?IF ( another subroutine handles it ) -> Catch, Next: that subroutine :ELSE -> fail to the caller
@CONDITION (HANDOFF): # per call ?IF ( the called work needs a fresh agent ) -> mark the call as a handoff; the executor issues its nonce when it runs (Tools: handoff) :ELSE -> run it in the same agent
@CONDITION (ENTRY): # per run ?IF ( {Element_Logic} is not empty ) -> runs start from the element logic :ELSE ?IF ( {Run_Args} is not NULL ) -> {Entry_Call} := the entry subroutine's call with {Run_Args} :ELSE -> submit only; do not start a run
@ROUTER: @MATCH ( {Stage} ): | [LOAD] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Load_Shared ) | [ORIENT] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Orient ) | [WRITE] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Write_Subroutines ) | [SUBMIT] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Submit ) | [START] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Start )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Load_Shared): [STEP 1]: FOR each section in {SHARED}: read it with metaform prompt <name> <version>. [STEP 2]: {Stage} := ORIENT. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Orient): [STEP 1]: ∇({Goal}) => what the workflow is, the kinds of elements it works on, the knowledge layers it needs, and whether it runs from element logic. [STEP 2]: List the existing knowledge layers and workflow elements (Tools: list layers, list workflows). Reuse what fits. [STEP 3]: Δ => {Workflow_Addr}; {Layers}. [STEP 4]: {Stage} := WRITE. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Write_Subroutines): [STEP 1]: ∇({Goal}) => the steps that reach it. [STEP 2]: FOR each step: APPLY ITERATION, FAILURE, HANDOFF. Δ => its content in Axiom: all six modules, PARAMs, acceptance, control flow, Retry, Catch, Raises, and RETURN or the next call. [STEP 3]: Δ => {Subroutines}; {Entry_Subroutine}. [STEP 4]: IF elements to run from are named: Δ => {Element_Logic}. [STEP 5]: Run @VERIFY. All pass -> {Stage} := SUBMIT. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Submit): [STEP 1]: SUBMIT the workflow element at {Workflow_Addr} into {Target_Layer}, with {Layers} (Tools: submit workflow). [STEP 2]: FOR each subroutine: SUBMIT its LOGIC on the workflow element (Tools: submit logic). [STEP 3]: FOR each item in {Element_Logic}: SUBMIT its LOGIC in a join on that element, in its layer (Tools: submit element logic). [STEP 4]: EXPORT `<workflow name>.metaform` from the stored workflow (Tools: export workflow). [STEP 5]: {Stage} := START. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Start): [STEP 1]: APPLY ENTRY. [STEP 2]: If there is an {Entry_Call}, hand it to the executor (Tools: run). Report what was submitted and how a run starts. YIELD.
@VERIFY: [V1] Was every section in {SHARED} read at its pinned version? NO -> {Stage} := LOAD. [V2] Is there one logic requirement per subroutine, and no flattened script? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V3] Is each subroutine a complete Axiom script with all six modules? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V4] Does every call name a subroutine that exists at its address? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V5] Does every subroutine declare its PARAMs, state its acceptance, and end in RETURN or a call? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V6] Is the entry subroutine named? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V7] Does every loop have MaxAttempts and say what it RETURNs when they run out? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V8] Does every subroutine list the errors it raises, does every Retry and Catch match a listed name or [any], does every Catch name a handler that exists, and can the caller of the failed subroutine use the handler's RETURN? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V9] Does every handed-off subroutine carry complete instructions, state the data it accepts and returns, and end in RETURN? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V10] Does any workflow subroutine's content hold a metaform address or a project-specific name, or read a project element from this instead of a parameter? YES -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V11] Does element logic pass this instead of a written-out address, read values that may be absent with .get, and never assign to or call through this? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V12] Is every layer in {Layers} one that exists? NO -> {Stage} := ORIENT. [V13] All pass? -> {Stage} := SUBMIT.

prompts.workflow-ingest:
# ============================================================ # AXIOM SCRIPT :: workflow-ingest (v1.7) # Compiled by Metaform Axiom-OS v1.1 # Turns an existing prompt or workflow into callable workflow logic. # ============================================================ # # This prompt is one instruction in two pieces: # - the shared sections pinned in {SHARED}: the Axiom language, the system workflows run in, # the namespace rules, the rules every workflow follows, the tools, and the delexicalization rules; # - your task, below. # Read the shared sections first, at their pinned versions. They bind as if written here. # # ============================================================
@STATE_INIT: # ---------- SHARED SECTIONS (pinned; read each with: metaform prompt <name> <version>) ---------- {SHARED} := [ axiom-language 1, metaform-system 1, namespace-rules 1, workflow-logic 1, metaform-tools 1, delexicalization-rules 1 ]
{Workflow_Text} := [the imported workflow or prompt, verbatim] {Target_Layer} := [the knowledge layer to write the workflow into, from whoever gave you this prompt] {Stage} := LOAD {Delexicalized} := NULL # {Workflow_Text} after delexicalization-rules {Workflow_Addr} := NULL # the workflow element, e.g. workflow.quality.code {Layers} := [] # knowledge layers the workflow says go with it, if any {Subroutines} := [] # one per step: name, PARAMs, acceptance, control flow, returns, raises {Entry_Subroutine} := NULL # the subroutine for the workflow's first step
@CONSTRAINT: # --- Shared sections --- +> READ every section in {SHARED} at its pinned version before anything else. Each binds as if written here. !> NEVER read a shared section at any version other than the one pinned.
# --- Fidelity --- !> NEVER paraphrase, reorder, summarize, or add to any instruction. A step's instructions go into its subroutine verbatim, as delexicalized. !> NEVER add a step, condition, loop, route or error the workflow does not state. +> DELEXICALIZE first, per delexicalization-rules. +> What the workflow leaves unstated becomes a parameter, passed by the caller: acceptance=PARAM, max_attempts=PARAM.
# --- Writing the workflow --- +> Follow workflow-logic for every workflow element and subroutine you write. +> A subroutine is named for what its step does. The entry subroutine is the workflow's first step. +> Each placeholder a step uses ({Element_A}, ...) is a PARAM of that step's subroutine. +> A subroutine's Raises: line lists only the errors the workflow states, or none. +> {Layers} holds the knowledge layers the workflow says go with it, if any.
# --- Control flow, as stated --- +> A stated branch is a Choice. Stated independent work is Parallel. A stated set of things is a Map. +> A stated retry is a Retry; a stated failure route is a Catch, Next: the subroutine it names.
# --- Handoffs --- +> A handoff exists wherever the workflow assigns work to a different agent or persona than the step before it, or calls for independence from prior work (e.g., "fresh eyes", independent review).
# --- The user and tools --- ~> If you are blocked on a decision that is the user's to make, ask the user (Tools: ask) and carry on with what does not depend on the answer. +> Use only the tools in metaform-tools, and follow its rule for tools marked <<TO FILL>>.
@CONDITION (ITERATION): # per step ?IF ( the wording works through a set: "each", "every", "all", a list ) -> Map, MaxConcurrency: 1 :ELSE -> Task
@CONDITION (ACCEPTANCE): # per step ?IF ( the wording states when the step is done ) -> that condition :ELSE -> acceptance := PARAM
@CONDITION (FAILURE): # per step ?IF ( the wording states what to do when it fails ) -> Retry / Catch as stated :ELSE -> fail to the caller
@CONDITION (NEXT): # per step ?IF ( the wording states a following step ) -> end with a call to that step's subroutine :ELSE -> RETURN what the step produces
@CONDITION (HANDOFF): # per step ?IF ( the agent changes, or the wording calls for independence ) -> the call into this step runs as a handoff by nonce :ELSE -> same agent
@ROUTER: @MATCH ( {Stage} ): | [LOAD] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Load_Shared ) | [DELEX] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Delexicalize ) | [SEGMENT] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Segment ) | [WRITE] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Write_Subroutines ) | [SUBMIT] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Submit )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Load_Shared): [STEP 1]: FOR each section in {SHARED}: read it with metaform prompt <name> <version>. [STEP 2]: {Stage} := DELEX. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Delexicalize): [STEP 1]: APPLY delexicalization-rules to {Workflow_Text}; {Delexicalized} := the result. [STEP 2]: {Stage} := SEGMENT. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Segment): [STEP 1]: ∇({Delexicalized}) => what the workflow is, its steps in order, each step's verbatim text and placeholders, and the knowledge layers it names. [STEP 2]: Δ => {Workflow_Addr} by namespace-rules; {Layers}. [STEP 3]: {Stage} := WRITE. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Write_Subroutines): [STEP 1]: FOR each step: APPLY ITERATION, ACCEPTANCE, FAILURE, NEXT, HANDOFF. Δ => its content in Axiom: all six modules, PARAMs, the verbatim instructions, acceptance, control flow, Raises, and RETURN or the next call. [STEP 2]: Δ => {Subroutines}; {Entry_Subroutine}. [STEP 3]: Run @VERIFY. All pass -> {Stage} := SUBMIT. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Submit): [STEP 1]: SUBMIT the workflow element at {Workflow_Addr} into {Target_Layer}, with {Layers} (Tools: submit workflow). [STEP 2]: FOR each subroutine: SUBMIT its LOGIC on the workflow element (Tools: submit logic). [STEP 3]: EXPORT `<workflow name>.metaform` from the stored workflow (Tools: export workflow). [STEP 4]: Report what was submitted and the entry subroutine's call form. YIELD.
@VERIFY: [V1] Was every section in {SHARED} read at its pinned version? NO -> {Stage} := LOAD. [V2] Is every step's text in its subroutine exactly as delexicalized? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V3] Is there one logic requirement per step, and no flattened script? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V4] Is each subroutine a complete Axiom script with all six modules? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V5] Does every call name a subroutine that exists at its address? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V6] Is every placeholder a step uses declared as a PARAM of its subroutine? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V7] Does any subroutine hold a step, condition, loop, route or error the workflow does not state, other than PARAMs for what it leaves unstated and the rules of workflow-logic? YES -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V8] Does every handed-off subroutine carry complete instructions, state the data it accepts and returns, and end in RETURN? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V9] Is the entry subroutine named? NO -> RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V10] Was any nonce requested during ingest? YES -> REVERT; RECURSE Write_Subroutines. [V11] All pass? -> {Stage} := SUBMIT.

prompts.prompt-lexicalization:
# ============================================================ # AXIOM SCRIPT :: prompt-lexicalization (v1.0) # Compiled by Metaform Axiom-OS v1.0 # ============================================================
@STATE_INIT: {Prompt_Text} := [the imported prompt or workflow, verbatim] {Map} := [this project's namespace, read via {MAP_READ}] {Subjects} := [] # each: span in {Prompt_Text}, canonical name, namespace address {Unresolved} := [] # spans that name something with no entry in {Map} {Output} := NULL
# ---------- OWNER PLACEHOLDERS (fill before use) ---------- {MAP_READ} := [MCP tool that reads map entries] {PROMPT_STORE} := [MCP tool that saves a lexicalized prompt] {UNRESOLVED_POLICY} := [keep wording | mark missing | stop and ask] {ADDRESS_PLACEMENT} := [inline after each name | one block of name -> address]
@CONSTRAINT: !> NEVER paraphrase, reorder, summarize, or add to any instruction. The only change is replacing a name. !> NEVER use the short name (name_length lookback). Use the entry's canonical `name` field. !> NEVER invent a name or address that is not in {Map}. +> Every subject the prompt names that has an entry in {Map} carries its canonical name and its namespace address. +> Match by what the wording refers to, not by exact spelling. +> Handle every span with no entry in {Map} per {UNRESOLVED_POLICY}.
@CONDITION (PLACEHOLDER_CHECK): ?IF ( any OWNER PLACEHOLDER is unfilled ) -> SAY which; HALT :ELSE -> CONTINUE
@CONDITION (MATCH): # per span that names something ?IF ( the span refers to an entry in {Map} ) -> RESOLVED :ELSE -> UNRESOLVED
@ROUTER: APPLY PLACEHOLDER_CHECK. EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Identify_Subjects ) FOR each span: @MATCH ( MATCH ): | [RESOLVED] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Replace_Name ) | [UNRESOLVED] -> EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Apply_Unresolved_Policy ) EXECUTE_PIPELINE( Assemble )
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Identify_Subjects): [STEP 1]: ∇({Prompt_Text}) => isolate every span that names something in the system. YIELD spans.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Replace_Name): [STEP 1]: ∇(span, {Map}) => isolate the entry's canonical name and namespace address. [STEP 2]: Δ => replace the span with the canonical name; APPEND (span, name, address) to {Subjects}. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Apply_Unresolved_Policy): [STEP 1]: APPEND span to {Unresolved}; act per {UNRESOLVED_POLICY}. YIELD.
@EXECUTE_PIPELINE (Assemble): [STEP 1]: Δ({Prompt_Text} with replacements, {Subjects}) => attach addresses per {ADDRESS_PLACEMENT}. [STEP 2]: {Output} := result; SAVE via {PROMPT_STORE}. YIELD.
@VERIFY: [V1] Is every difference between {Prompt_Text} and {Output} a name replacement or an attached address? NO -> RECURSE Assemble from the original text. [V2] Does every name in {Subjects} match its entry's canonical `name` field exactly? NO -> RECURSE Replace_Name. [V3] Is every span in {Unresolved} handled per {UNRESOLVED_POLICY}? NO -> RECURSE Apply_Unresolved_Policy. [V4] All pass? YES -> YIELD {Output}.
